<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Transition Matching</h2>

<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by describing the framework of Transition Matching (TM), which can be seen as a simplified and general discrete time formulation for diffusion/flow models. Then, we focus on several, unexplored TM design choices and instantiations that goes beyond diffusion/flow models. In particular: we consider more powerful transition kernels and/or discontinuous noise-to-data processes. In the experiments section we show these choices lead to state-of-the-art image generation methods.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>General framework</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Notation</span>
We use capital letters <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X,Y,Z,A,B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X,Y,Z,A,B</annotation></semantics></math> to denote random variables (RVs) and lower-case letter <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x,y,z,a,b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x,y,z,a,b</annotation></semantics></math> to denote their particular states. One exception is time <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> where we abuse notation a bit and use it to denote both particular times and a RV. All our variables and states reside in euclidean spaces <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. The probability density function (PDF) of a random variable <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y}(x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y}(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. For RVs <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (and only for them) we use the simpler PDF notation <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. We use the standard notations for joints <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X,Y}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X,Y}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> and conditional densities <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{X|Y}(x|y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{X|Y}(x|y)</annotation></semantics></math> densities. We denote <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T]=\left\{0,1,\ldots,T\right\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T]=\left\{0,1,\ldots,T\right\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Problem definition</span>
Given a training set of i.i.d. samples from an unknown <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target distribution</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>, and some easy to sample <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">source distribution</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our goal is to learn a Markov Process, defined by a <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">probability transition kernel</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t+1|t}^{\theta}(x_{t+1}|x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t+1|t}^{\theta}(x_{t+1}|x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T-1]</annotation></semantics></math> taking us from <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}\sim p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}\sim p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, we define a series of random variables <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t})_{t\in[T]}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t})_{t\in[T]}</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> and</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{t+1}\sim p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t})\text{ for all }t\in[T-1]\text{ then }X_{T}\sim p_{T}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mtext> for all </mtext><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext> then </mtext><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t+1}\sim p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t})\text{ for all }t\in[T-1]\text{ then }X_{T}\sim p_{T}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:30%;">
<div id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:166.6pt;height:43.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="S2.F2.3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.F2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S2.F2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="59.61" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 225.92 59.61" width="225.92"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,59.61) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(14.26,0) translate(0,29.8)"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#E6E6FF;" fill="#E6E6FF" stroke="#000000"><path d="M 13.98 0 C 13.98 7.72 7.72 13.98 0 13.98 C -7.72 13.98 -13.98 7.72 -13.98 0 C -13.98 -7.72 -7.72 -13.98 0 -13.98 C 7.72 -13.98 13.98 -7.72 13.98 0 Z M 0 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -10.63 -3.69)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:1.54em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.15em;font-size:10pt;" height="11.53" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="21.27"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F2.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#E6FFE6;" fill="#E6FFE6"><path d="M 211.39 0 C 211.39 16.31 176.13 29.53 132.65 29.53 C 89.16 29.53 53.91 16.31 53.91 0 C 53.91 -16.31 89.16 -29.53 132.65 -29.53 C 176.13 -29.53 211.39 -16.31 211.39 0 Z M 132.65 0"></path></g><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 97.83 -3.46)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:5.03em;--ltx-fo-height:0.7em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.2em;font-size:10pt;" height="12.45" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.69)" width="69.64"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><math id="S2.F2.pic1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0},\ldots,X_{T-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0},\ldots,X_{T-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 14.26 0 L 52.51 0"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linejoin="miter" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 52.51 0)"><path d="M -7.23 3.24 L 0.42 0 L -7.23 -3.24 Z"></path></g></g></svg></span></span></p>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Supervising process.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervising process</span>
Training such a Markov process is done with the help of a <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">supervising process</em>, which is a stochastic process <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{0},X_{1},\ldots,X_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{0},X_{1},\ldots,X_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> defined given data samples <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> using a <em id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">conditional process</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0,\ldots,T-1|T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0,\ldots,T-1|T}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0,\ldots,T}(x_{0},\ldots,x_{T})=q_{0,\ldots,T-1|T}(x_{0},\ldots,x_{T-1}|x_{T})p_{T}(x_{T})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0,\ldots,T}(x_{0},\ldots,x_{T})=q_{0,\ldots,T-1|T}(x_{0},\ldots,x_{T-1}|x_{T})p_{T}(x_{T}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">and <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{0,\ldots,T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{0,\ldots,T}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the joint probability of the supervising process <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t})_{t\in T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t})_{t\in T}</annotation></semantics></math>. The only constraint on the conditional process is that its marginal at time <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> is the easy to sample distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{0}=p_{0}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{0}=p_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.3" class="ltx_p">Note that this definition is very general and allows, for example, arbitrary non-continuous processes, and indeed we utilize such a process below. Transition matching engages with the supervising process <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t})_{t\in T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t})_{t\in T}</annotation></semantics></math> by sampling pairs of consecutive states <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t},X_{t+1})\sim q_{t,t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},X_{t+1})\sim q_{t,t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T-1]</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss</span> The model <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained to transition between consecutive states <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}\rightarrow X_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}\rightarrow X_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> in the sense of equation <a href="#S2.E1" title="Equation 1 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> by regressing <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math> defined from the supervising process <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math>. This motivates the loss utilizing a distance/divergence <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> between distributions</p>
<table id="S2.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{t}}D\big(q_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t}),p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t})\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{t}}D\big(q_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t}),p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled uniformly from <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[T-1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[T-1]</annotation></semantics></math>. However, this loss requires evaluating <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math> which is usually hard to compute. Therefore, to make the training tractable we require that the distance <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> has an <em id="S2.SS1.p4.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empirical form</em>, i.e., can be expressed as an expectation of an empirical one-sample loss <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}</annotation></semantics></math> over target samples. We define the loss</p>
<table id="S2.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{t}}\overbrace{\mathbb{E}_{X_{t+1}}\hat{D}(X_{t+1},p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t}))}^{D\text{ in empirical form}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{t},X_{t+1}}\hat{D}\big(X_{t+1},p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t})\big)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mover><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>p</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msup><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏞</mo></mover><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> in empirical form</mtext></mrow></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{t}}\overbrace{\mathbb{E}_{X_{t+1}}\hat{D}(X_{t+1},p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t}))}^{D\text{ in empirical form}}=\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{t},X_{t+1}}\hat{D}\big(X_{t+1},p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}(\cdot|X_{t})\big),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t},X_{t+1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},X_{t+1})</annotation></semantics></math> are sampled from the joint <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t,t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t,t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> with the help of equation <a href="#S2.E2" title="Equation 2 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, namely, first sample data <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}\sim p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}\sim p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(X_{t},X_{t+1})\sim q_{t,t+1|T}(\cdot|X_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},X_{t+1})\sim q_{t,t+1|T}(\cdot|X_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>. Notably, equation <a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> can be used to learn arbitrary transition kernels, in contrast to e.g., Gaussian kernels used in discrete time diffusion models or deterministic kernels used in flow matching. The particular choice of the cost <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> depends on the modeling paradigm chosen for the transition kernel <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math>, and discussed later.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.SS1.fig1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="alg1" class="ltx_float ltx_figure_panel ltx_float_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span> Transition Matching Training</figcaption>
<div id="alg1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg1.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg1.l1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="width:85.4pt;"><math id="alg1.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;" mathcolor="#00FFFF" mathsize="0.800em">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;"> Data</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t,Y|T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t,Y|T}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg1.l2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="width:85.4pt;"><math id="alg1.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;" mathcolor="#00FFFF" mathsize="0.800em">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;"> Process</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg1.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg1.l3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="width:85.4pt;"><math id="alg1.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;" mathcolor="#00FFFF" mathsize="0.800em">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;"> Number of TM steps</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span> not converged <span id="alg1.l4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
                Sample <math id="alg1.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\sim\mathcal{U}([T\!-\!1])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒰</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo lspace="0.052em">−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\sim\mathcal{U}([T\!-\!1])</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="alg1.l5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}\sim p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}\sim p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
                Sample <math id="alg1.l6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(X_{t},Y)\sim q_{t,Y|T}(\cdot|X_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},Y)\sim q_{t,Y|T}(\cdot|X_{T})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow\hat{D}(Y,p^{\theta}_{Y|t}(\cdot|X_{t}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}(\theta)\leftarrow\hat{D}(Y,p^{\theta}_{Y|t}(\cdot|X_{t}))</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg1.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                <math id="alg1.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta\leftarrow\theta-\gamma\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mi>θ</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><msub><mo rspace="0.167em">∇</mo><mi>θ</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta\leftarrow\theta-\gamma\nabla_{\theta}\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg1.l8.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg1.l8.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="width:85.4pt;"><math id="alg1.l8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;" mathcolor="#00FFFF" mathsize="0.800em">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg1.l8.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;"> Optimization step</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg1.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">while</span>
</div>
<div id="alg1.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg1.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg1.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg1.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_figure_panel ltx_float_algorithm ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle ltx_framed ltx_framed_top" style="width:165.6pt;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> Transition Matching Sampling</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">1:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg2.l1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l1.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg2.l1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="width:85.4pt;"><math id="alg2.l1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;" mathcolor="#00FFFF" mathsize="0.800em">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l1.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;"> Source distribution</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">2:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{Y|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{Y|t}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l2.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg2.l2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="width:85.4pt;"><math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;" mathcolor="#00FFFF" mathsize="0.800em">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;"> Trained model</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">3:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t,Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t,Y}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l3.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg2.l3.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="width:85.4pt;"><math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;" mathcolor="#00FFFF" mathsize="0.800em">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l3.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;"> Parametrization</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">4:</span></span>
                
                
              <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l4.3" class="ltx_text" style="float:right;"><span id="alg2.l4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_align_left ltx_inline-block" style="width:85.4pt;"><math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\triangleright" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;" mathcolor="#00FFFF" mathsize="0.800em">⊳</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\triangleright</annotation></semantics></math><span id="alg2.l4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FFFF;"> Number of TM steps</span></span>
</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">5:</span></span>
                
                
              Sample <math id="alg2.l5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim p_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim p_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">6:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg2.l6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l7.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">7:</span></span>
                
                
                Sample <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y\sim p^{\theta}_{Y|t}(\cdot|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\sim p^{\theta}_{Y|t}(\cdot|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l8.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">8:</span></span>
                
                
                Sample <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{t+1}\sim q_{t+1|t,Y}(\cdot|X_{t},Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t+1}\sim q_{t+1|t,Y}(\cdot|X_{t},Y)</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l9.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">9:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline"><span id="alg2.l10.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">10:</span></span>
                
                
              <span id="alg2.l10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure></div></div>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kernel parameterizations</span> The first and natural option to parameterize <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{t+1|t}^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{t+1|t}^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is to regress <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math> directly as is done in equation <a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. This turns out to be a good modeling choice in certain cases. However, in some cases one can use other parameterizations that turn out to be beneficial, as is also done for flow and diffusion models. To do that in the general case, we use the <em id="S2.SS1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">law of total probability</em> applied to the conditional probabilities <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math> with some latent RV <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S2.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t}(x_{t+1}|x_{t})=\int q_{t+1|t,Y}(x_{t+1}|x_{t},y)q_{Y|t}(y|x_{t})\mathrm{d}y," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><mo>∫</mo><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0em">𝑑</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t}(x_{t+1}|x_{t})=\int q_{t+1|t,Y}(x_{t+1}|x_{t},y)q_{Y|t}(y|x_{t})\mathrm{d}y,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{Y|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{Y|t}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <em id="S2.SS1.p5.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">posterior distribution</em> of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=x_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=x_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t,Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t,Y}</annotation></semantics></math> is easy to sample (often a deterministic function of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>). Then the posterior of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is set as the new target of the learning process instead of the transition kernel. That is, instead of the loss in equation <a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> we consider</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{t},Y}\hat{D}\big(Y,p^{\theta}_{Y|t}(\cdot|X_{t})\big)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle{\mathcal{L}}(\theta)=\mathbb{E}_{t,X_{t},Y}\hat{D}\big(Y,p^{\theta}_{Y|t}(\cdot|X_{t})\big).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.3" class="ltx_p">Similarly, during training, sampling from the joint <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{t},Y)\sim q_{t,Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},Y)\sim q_{t,Y}</annotation></semantics></math>, is accomplished by first sampling data <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="(X_{t},Y)\sim q_{t,Y|T}(\cdot|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{t},Y)\sim q_{t,Y|T}(\cdot|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Once the posterior <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{Y|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{Y|t}</annotation></semantics></math> is trained, sampling from the transition <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math> during inference is done with the help of equation <a href="#S2.E6" title="Equation 6 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>.
To summarize, in cases we want to use non-trivial kernel parameterization, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\neq X_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>≠</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\neq X_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>, we sample from <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t,Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t,Y}</annotation></semantics></math> (in sampling) and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t,Y|T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t,Y|T}</annotation></semantics></math> (in training). See Algorithms <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> the training and sampling pseudocodes.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Kernel modeling</span> Once a desirable <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is identified, the remaining part is to choose a generative model for the kernel <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{Y|t}^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{Y|t}^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Importantly, one of the key advantages in TM comes from choosing expressive kernels that result in more elaborate transition kernels than used previously.
A kernel modeling is set by a choice of a <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">probability model</em> for <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{Y|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{Y|t}</annotation></semantics></math> and a <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">loss</em> to learn it. We denote the probability model choice by <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B|A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B|A</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">condition</em> and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target</em>. For example, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y|X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y|X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> will denote a model that predicts a sample of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> given a sample of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. We will also use more elaborate probability models, such as autoregressive models. To this end, consider the state <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> reshaped into individual <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">tokens</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=(Y^{1},\ldots,Y^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=(Y^{1},\ldots,Y^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and then <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{i}|\left(Y^{&lt;i},X_{t}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{i}|\left(Y^{&lt;i},X_{t}\right)</annotation></semantics></math> means that our model samples the token <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> given previous tokens of <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y^{&lt;i}=(Y^{1},\ldots,Y^{i-1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y^{&lt;i}=(Y^{1},\ldots,Y^{i-1})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">All our models are learned with flow matching (FM) loss. For completeness, we provide the key components of flow matching formulated generically to learn to sample from <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B|A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B|A</annotation></semantics></math>. Individual states of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>A</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> are denoted <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>b</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Flow matching models <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{B|A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{B|A}</annotation></semantics></math> via a velocity field <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}(b|a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}(b|a)</annotation></semantics></math> that is used to sample from <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="p^{\theta}_{B|A}(\cdot|a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{B|A}(\cdot|a)</annotation></semantics></math> by solving the Ordinary Differential Equation (ODE)</p>
<table id="S2.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\mathrm{d}B_{s}}{\mathrm{d}s}=u_{s}^{\theta}(B_{s}|a)" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\mathrm{d}B_{s}}{\mathrm{d}s}=u_{s}^{\theta}(B_{s}|a)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.2" class="ltx_p">initializing with a sample <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> (the standard normal distribution) and solving until <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=1</annotation></semantics></math>. In turn, <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the desired sample, i.e., <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B_{1}\sim p^{\theta}_{B|A}(\cdot|a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{1}\sim p^{\theta}_{B|A}(\cdot|a)</annotation></semantics></math>. The loss <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math>, used to train FM, has an empirical form and minimizes the difference between <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{B|A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{B|A}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{B|A}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{B|A}</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="S2.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\hat{D}\big(B,p^{\theta}_{B|A}(\cdot|a)\big)=\mathbb{E}_{s,B_{0}}\left\|u_{s}^{\theta}(B_{s}|a)-(B-B_{0})\right\|^{2}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>D</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mrow><mo>‖</mo><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{D}\big(B,p^{\theta}_{B|A}(\cdot|a)\big)=\mathbb{E}_{s,B_{0}}\left\|u_{s}^{\theta}(B_{s}|a)-(B-B_{0})\right\|^{2},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> is sampled uniformly in <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{0}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="B\sim q_{B|A}(\cdot|a)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\sim q_{B|A}(\cdot|a)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{s}=(1-s)B_{0}+sB" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{s}=(1-s)B_{0}+sB</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the key design choices in Transition Matching:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center">TM design:</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervising process</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parametrization</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modeling</span></span></span>
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS1.p9.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B|A" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B|A</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Transition Matching made practical</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key contribution of this paper is identifying previously unexplored design choices in the TM framework that results in effective generative models. We focus on two TM variants: Difference Transition Matching (DTM), and Autoregressive Transition Matching (ARTM/FHTM).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Difference Transition Matching</span> Our first instance of TM makes the following choices:</p><span id="S2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center">DTM:</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervising process</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parametrization</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modeling</span></span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> linear</span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X_{T}-X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X_{T}-X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=Y\,|\,A=(t,X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="false" lspace="0.448em">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=Y\,|\,A=(t,X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.3" class="ltx_p">As the supervising process <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> we use the standard <em id="S2.SS2.p2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">linear process</em> (a.k.a., Conditional Optimal Transport), defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=\left(1-\frac{t}{T}\right)X_{0}+\frac{t}{T}X_{T},\qquad t\in[T]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="2.167em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=\left(1-\frac{t}{T}\right)X_{0}+\frac{t}{T}X_{T},\qquad t\in[T],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}\sim p_{0}={\mathcal{N}}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}\sim p_{0}={\mathcal{N}}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}\sim p_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}\sim p_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the same process used in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. For the kernel parameterization <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> we will use the <em id="S2.SS2.p2.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">difference latent</em> (see Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, left),</p>
<table id="S2.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X_{T}-X_{0}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X_{T}-X_{0}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23589v1/tm_fm.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:108/30;" width="108" height="30"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Difference prediction given <math id="S2.F3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> (left) and flow matching velocity <math id="S2.F3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{t}(X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{t}(X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> (right).
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">During training, sampling <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{t,Y|T}(\cdot|X_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t,Y|T}(\cdot|X_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e., given <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>) is done by sampling <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>, and using <a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and <a href="#S2.E11" title="Equation 11 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> to compute <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t},Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t},Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the definition in <a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> and rearranging gives</p>
<table id="S2.E12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t+1}=X_{t}+\frac{1}{T}Y," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t+1}=X_{t}+\frac{1}{T}Y,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">and this equation can be used to sample from <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{t+1|t,Y}(\cdot|X_{t},Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t,Y}(\cdot|X_{t},Y)</annotation></semantics></math> during inference. See Figure <a href="#S2.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for an illustration of a sampled path from this supervising process. We learn to sample from the posterior <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{Y|t}\approx q_{Y|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo>≈</mo><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{Y|t}\approx q_{Y|t}</annotation></semantics></math> using flow matching with <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(t,X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(t,X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=Y</annotation></semantics></math>. This means we learn a velocity field <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}(y|t,x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}(y|t,x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> and train it with Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and the CFM loss in equation <a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. Note that in this case one can also learn a continuous time <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[0,T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[0,T]</annotation></semantics></math> which allows more flexible sampling.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:22%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23589v1/dtm_path.svg" id="S2.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:23/14;" width="23" height="14"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S2.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">DTM path, eq. <a href="#S2.E12" title="Equation 12 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The last remaining component is choosing the architecture for <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Let <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x^{1},\ldots,x^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x^{1},\ldots,x^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> be a reshaped state to <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. For example, each <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> can represent a patch in an image <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>. Next, note that in each transition step we need to sample <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Y\sim p^{\theta}_{Y|t}(\cdot|X_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\sim p^{\theta}_{Y|t}(\cdot|X_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> by approximating the solution of the ODE in equation <a href="#S2.E8" title="Equation 8 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. Therefore, to keep the sampling process efficient, we follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and use a small <em id="S2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">head</em> <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> that generates all tokens in a batch and is fed with latents from a large <em id="S2.SS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backbone</em> <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our velocity model is defined as</p>
<table id="S2.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}(y|t,x_{t})=\left[g_{s,t}^{\theta}(y^{1},h_{t}^{1}),\ldots,g_{s,t}^{\theta}(y^{n},h_{t}^{n})\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}(y|t,x_{t})=\left[g_{s,t}^{\theta}(y^{1},h_{t}^{1}),\ldots,g_{s,t}^{\theta}(y^{n},h_{t}^{n})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>-th output token of the backbone, i.e., <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[h_{t}^{1},h_{t}^{2},\ldots,h_{t}^{n}]=f_{t}^{\theta}(x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[h_{t}^{1},h_{t}^{2},\ldots,h_{t}^{n}]=f_{t}^{\theta}(x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>. See Figure <a href="#S2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (DTM) for an illustration of this architecture. One limitation of this architecture worth mentioning is that in each transition step, each token <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated independently, which limits the power of this kernel. We discuss this in the experiments section but nevertheless demonstrate that DTM with this architecture still leads to state-of-the-art image generation model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Connection to flow matching</span>
Although flow matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Albergo and Vanden-Eijnden, 2022</a>)</cite> is a <em id="S2.SS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">deterministic</em> process while DTM samples from a stochastic transition kernel in each step, a connection between the two is revealed by noting that the <em id="S2.SS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">expectation</em> of a DTM step coincides with Flow Matching Euler step, i.e.,</p>
<table id="S2.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[Y|X_{t}=x\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[X_{T}-X_{0}|X_{t}=x\right]=u_{t}(x)," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\left[Y|X_{t}=x\right]=\mathbb{E}\left[X_{T}-X_{0}|X_{t}=x\right]=u_{t}(x),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p">which is exactly the marginal velocity in flow matching, see Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. In fact, as <math id="S2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math> (or equivalently, steps are getting smaller), DTM is becoming more and more deterministic, converging to FM with Euler step, providing a novel and unexpected elementary proof (i.e., without the continuity equation) for FM marginal velocity. In Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Convergence of DTM to flow matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> we prove</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1</span></span><span id="Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(informal)
As the number of steps increases, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\rightarrow\infty" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\rightarrow\infty</annotation></semantics></math>, DTM converges to Euler step FM,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t+k}\approx x_{t}+\frac{k}{T}\mathbb{E}\left[X_{T}-X_{0}|X_{t}=x_{t}\right]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≈</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>k</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t+k}\approx x_{t}+\frac{k}{T}\mathbb{E}\left[X_{T}-X_{0}|X_{t}=x_{t}\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">as <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k/T\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k/T\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\ell}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\ell}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\forall\ell&gt;t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">ℓ</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\forall\ell&gt;t</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> with a optimally trained <math id="Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p^{\theta}_{Y|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p^{\theta}_{Y|t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p7.1" class="ltx_p">We attribute the empirical success of DTM over flow matching to its more elaborate kernel.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S2.F5.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S2.F5.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.F5.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23589v1/arch_dtm.svg" id="S2.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:117/62;" width="117" height="62"></object></td>
<td id="S2.F5.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23589v1/arch_artm.svg" id="S2.F5.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:172/67;" width="172" height="67"></object></td>
<td id="S2.F5.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23589v1/arch_fhtm.svg" id="S2.F5.g3" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:157/67;" width="157" height="67"></object></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.F5.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.F5.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">DTM</td>
<td id="S2.F5.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">ARTM</td>
<td id="S2.F5.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center">FHTM</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S2.F5.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Architectures of the methods suggested in the paper. Backbone (orange) is the main network (transformer); head (green) is a small network (2% backbone parameters); blue tokens use full attention, gray tokens are causal; <math id="S2.F5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is the output velocity.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Autoregressive Transition Matching</span> Our second instance of TM is geared towards incorporating state-of-the-art media generation in autoregressive models, and utilizes the following choices:</p><span id="S2.SS2.p8.2" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2"><span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center">ARTM:</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervising process</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parametrization</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modeling</span></span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> independent linear</span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p8.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=Y^{i}\,|\,A=(t,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=Y^{i}\,|\,A=(t,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F6" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2506.23589v1/linear_dependent_independent.svg" id="S2.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:110/34;" width="110" height="34"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S2.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Linear process (left) and independent linear process (right) showing possible <math id="S2.F6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> given a sample <math id="S2.F6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. The independent process has much wider support for <math id="S2.F6.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> given <math id="S2.F6.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p">In this case we use a novel supervising process we call <em id="S2.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">independent linear process</em>, defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=\left(1-\frac{t}{T}\right)X_{0,t}+\frac{t}{T}X_{T},\ t\in[T]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>t</mi><mi>T</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.667em">,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=\left(1-\frac{t}{T}\right)X_{0,t}+\frac{t}{T}X_{T},\ t\in[T],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p9.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0,t}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0,t}\sim{\mathcal{N}}(0,I)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t\in[T]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t\in[T]</annotation></semantics></math> are all i.i.d. samples. Sampling <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{t,t+1|T}(\cdot|X_{T})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t,t+1|T}(\cdot|X_{T})</annotation></semantics></math> is done by sampling <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0,t}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0,t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0,t+1}</annotation></semantics></math> and using <a href="#S2.E15" title="Equation 15 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>. Although the independent linear process has the same marginals <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> as the linear process in equation <a href="#S2.E10" title="Equation 10 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, it enjoys better regularity of the conditional <math id="S2.SS2.p9.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="q_{t+1|t}(\cdot|x_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t}(\cdot|x_{t})</annotation></semantics></math>, see Figure <a href="#S2.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for an illustration, and as demonstrated later in experiments is key for building state-of-the-art Autoregressive image generation models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p">For the transition kernel we use an Autoregressive
(AR) model with the choice of <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math>. As before, we let a state written as series of tokens <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x=(x^{1},\ldots,x^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x=(x^{1},\ldots,x^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and write the target kernel <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math> using the probability chain rule (as usual in AR modeling),</p>
<table id="S2.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{t+1|t}(X_{t+1}|X_{t})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}q_{t+1|t}^{i}(X_{t+1}^{i}|X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{t+1|t}(X_{t+1}|X_{t})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}q_{t+1|t}^{i}(X_{t+1}^{i}|X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p10.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t+1}^{&lt;1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t+1}^{&lt;1}</annotation></semantics></math> is the empty state. We will learn to sample from <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{i}_{t+1|t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{i}_{t+1|t}</annotation></semantics></math> using FM with <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(t,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(t,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=X_{t+1}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=X_{t+1}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. That is, we learn a velocity field <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}(y^{i}|t,x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}(y^{i}|t,x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math> trained with the CFM loss in equation <a href="#S2.E9" title="Equation 9 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>. This method builds upon the initial idea <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>)</cite> that uses such AR modeling to map in a single transition step from <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math> using diffusion, and in that sense ARTM is a generalization of that method.
Lastly the architecture for <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> is based on a similar construction to DTM with a few, rather minor changes. Using the same notation for the head <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>g</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> and backbone <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f^{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f^{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> models we define</p>
<table id="S2.E16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}(y^{i}|t,x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})=g_{s,t}^{\theta}(y^{i},h_{t+1}^{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}(y^{i}|t,x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})=g_{s,t}^{\theta}(y^{i},h_{t+1}^{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p10.3" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS2.p10.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t+1}^{i}=f_{t}^{\theta}(x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>f</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t+1}^{i}=f_{t}^{\theta}(x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (ARTM) shows an illustration of this architecture.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full-History ARTM</span> We consider a variant of ARTM that allows full "teacher-forcing" training and consequently provides a good candidate to be incorporated into multimodal AR model.</p><span id="S2.SS2.p11.2" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2"><span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center">FHTM:</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervising process</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parametrization</span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modeling</span></span></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{\leq t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{\leq t}</annotation></semantics></math> independent linear</span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X_{t+1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X_{t+1}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S2.SS2.p11.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=Y^{i}\,|\,A=(X_{0},\ldots,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>Y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=Y^{i}\,|\,A=(X_{0},\ldots,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span>
<p id="S2.SS2.p11.3" class="ltx_p">The idea is to use the full history of states, namely considering the kernel</p>
<table id="S2.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_eqnarray ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle q_{t+1|0,\ldots,t}(X_{t+1}|X_{0},\ldots,X_{t})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}q_{t+1|0,\ldots,t}^{i}(X_{t+1}^{i}|X_{0},\ldots,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∏</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle q_{t+1|0,\ldots,t}(X_{t+1}|X_{0},\ldots,X_{t})=\prod_{i=1}^{n}q_{t+1|0,\ldots,t}^{i}(X_{t+1}^{i}|X_{0},\ldots,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p11.4" class="ltx_p">and train an FM sampler from <math id="S2.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q^{i}_{t+1|0,\ldots,t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q^{i}_{t+1|0,\ldots,t}</annotation></semantics></math> with the choices <math id="S2.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A=(X_{0},\ldots,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A=(X_{0},\ldots,X_{t},X_{t+1}^{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math> (no need to add time <math id="S2.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> due to the full state sequence) and <math id="S2.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=X_{t+1}^{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=X_{t+1}^{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. The architecture of the velocity <math id="S2.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined by</p>
<table id="S2.E18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="u_{s}^{\theta}(y^{i}|x_{0},\ldots,x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})=g_{s}^{\theta}(y^{i},h_{t+1}^{i})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>u</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>g</mi><mi>s</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_{s}^{\theta}(y^{i}|x_{0},\ldots,x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})=g_{s}^{\theta}(y^{i},h_{t+1}^{i}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.p11.5" class="ltx_p">with <math id="S2.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{t+1}^{i}=f^{\theta}(x_{0},\ldots,x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>h</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>i</mi></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>θ</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{t+1}^{i}=f^{\theta}(x_{0},\ldots,x_{t},x_{t+1}^{&lt;i})</annotation></semantics></math> and we take <math id="S2.SS2.p11.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> to be fully causal. See Figure <a href="#S2.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> (FHTM).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Related work</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Diffusion and flows</span> We draw the connection to previous works from the perspective of transition matching. Diffusion models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sohl-Dickstein et al., 2015</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2023</a>)</cite> can be seen as an instance of TM by choosing <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> in the loss (<a href="#S2.E5" title="Equation 5 ‣ 2.1 General framework ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) to be the KL divergence, derived in diffusion literature as the variational lower bound <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2013</a>)</cite>. The popular <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math>-prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite> in transition matching formulation is achieved by the design choices</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para"><span id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_inline-logical-block ltx_framed ltx_framed_rectangle">
<span id="S3.p2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<span class="ltx_tbody">
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column ltx_th_row ltx_rowspan ltx_rowspan_2"><span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text"><math id="S3.p2.p1.m1" class="ltx_centering" alttext="{\epsilon}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\epsilon}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_align_center">-prediction:</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Supervising process</span></span>
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parametrization</span></span>
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_th ltx_th_column"><span id="S3.p2.p1.1.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Modeling</span></span></span>
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.p2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{t}=\sigma_{t}X_{0}+\alpha_{t}X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{t}=\sigma_{t}X_{0}+\alpha_{t}X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.p2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X_{0}</annotation></semantics></math></span>
<span id="S3.p2.p1.1.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="S3.p2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y|X_{t}\sim{\mathcal{N}}\left(Y|\ {\epsilon}_{t}^{\theta}(X_{t}),w_{t}^{2}I\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.500em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>t</mi><mi>θ</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y|X_{t}\sim{\mathcal{N}}\left(Y|\ {\epsilon}_{t}^{\theta}(X_{t}),w_{t}^{2}I\right)</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span>
</span>
</span></span>
<p id="S3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\sigma_{t},\alpha_{t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\sigma_{t},\alpha_{t})</annotation></semantics></math> is the scheduler, and non-zero <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> reproduces the sampling algorithm in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ho et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, while taking the limit <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{t}\rightarrow 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{t}\rightarrow 0</annotation></semantics></math> yields the sampling of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2022</a>)</cite>. Similarly, <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>-prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma et al., 2023</a>)</cite> is achieved by the parametrization <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=X_{T}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>T</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=X_{T}</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast to these work, our TM instantiations use more expressive kernel modeling. Relation to flow matching<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipman et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Albergo and Vanden-Eijnden, 2022</a>)</cite> is discussed in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Transition Matching made practical ‣ 2 Transition Matching ‣ Transition Matching: Scalable and Flexible Generative Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Generator matching <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holderrieth et al., 2025</a>)</cite> generalizes diffusion and flow models to general continuous time Markov process modeled with arbitrary generators, while we focus on discrete time Markov processes.
Another line of works adopted supervision processes that transition between different resolutions; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> used flow matching with a particular coupling between different resolution as the kernel modeling; <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> implemented a similar scheme but allowed the FM to be dependent on the previous states (frames) in an AR manner.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Autoregressive image generation</span> Early progress in text-to-image generation was achieved using autoregressive models over discrete latent spaces <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ramesh et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ding et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, with recent advances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tian et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sun et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite> claiming to surpass flow-based approaches. A complementary line of work explores autoregressive modeling directly in continuous space <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tschannen et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, demonstrating some advantages over discrete methods. In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fan et al., 2024</a>)</cite> this direction is scaled further, achieving SOTA results. In our experiments we compare these models in controlled setting and show that our autoregressive transition matching variants improves upon these models and achieves SOTA text-to-image performance with a fully causal architecture.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div id="S3.F7.3" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:506.1pt;vertical-align:-251.9pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-206.2pt,302.5pt) scale(0.45554577770734,0.45554577770734) ;">
<table id="S3.F7.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<tbody class="ltx_tbody">
<tr id="S3.F7.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<th id="S3.F7.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_th ltx_th_row" style="padding:1pt 4.0pt;">
<span id="S3.F7.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:86.3pt;">
